---
import PageNavigation from '~/components/nav/PageNavigation.astro'
import ResearchPanel from '~/components/research/ResearchPanel.astro'
import Link from '~/components/base/Link.astro'
import { withBasePath } from '~/utils/path'
---

<div class="prose mx-auto">
  <ResearchPanel
    label="At a glance"
    title="An independent publication with clear authorship"
    titleLevel="h2"
    compact
  >
    <ul>
      <li>
        Founded by Srinivas Medida (Sri), who remains the principal author and
        whose research is the publication's flagship body of work
      </li>
      <li>
        The Research archive records explicit horizons, catalysts, invalidation
        conditions and a retained Decision Log
      </li>
      <li>
        Selected Contributor Research published under each student's own name,
        with sources, limitations and disclosures
      </li>
      <li>
        Models and empirical work with reviewable assumptions and reproducible
        files where licensing permits
      </li>
    </ul>
  </ResearchPanel>

  <h2>What SM Research Is</h2>

  <p>
    I started SM Research as a place to record questions I was working through
    across markets, valuation and capital allocation. It remains an independent,
    student-led publication, and my research is its flagship body of work.
  </p>

  <p>
    Writing a view down makes my reasoning reviewable. It exposes the
    assumptions, gives readers something specific to challenge and forces me to
    state what would change the conclusion before the outcome is known. That
    record matters more to me than a polished catalogue of correct calls.
  </p>

  <h2>Founder Research</h2>

  <p>
    Everything under <Link href={withBasePath('/research/')}>Research</Link> is my
    work. This archive alone informs my Current Views, investment stances, historical
    thesis outcomes and Decision Log. I keep the original thesis visible alongside
    the evidence that followed.
  </p>

  <p>
    I am not trying to present a catalogue of successful calls. I want the
    archive to show how I frame questions, test evidence, size uncertainty and
    revise conclusions when the facts change. Failed theses stay published
    because removing them would create a cleaner record, but a less credible
    one.
  </p>

  <h2>Contributor Research</h2>

  <p>
    Investing offers many ways to understand how capital, risk and exchange
    shape real outcomes. I opened <Link href={withBasePath('/contributors/')}>
      Contributor Research</Link
    > to give other students a place to test serious ideas and publish selected work
    under their own names. Each article names its author beside the title and links
    to a simple index of that contributor's research.
  </p>

  <p>
    We can help sharpen structure, sourcing, presentation and analytical
    clarity. We do not replace the author's thesis, core evidence or underlying
    analysis. Contributors remain responsible for their conclusions, and their
    work does not enter my Current Views or Decision Log.
  </p>

  <h2>Analytical Standards</h2>

  <p>
    Work should begin with a defined analytical question rather than a
    predetermined conclusion. Facts, assumptions and interpretation should
    remain visibly separate. Evidence should be traceable, with primary and
    institutional sources preferred where available. Material risks,
    counterarguments, model limitations and relevant conflicts belong in the
    research itself.
  </p>

  <p>
    Models are useful when they make assumptions clearer or scenarios more
    testable. They are not used to add complexity for its own sake. Material
    errors should be corrected and revisions dated so readers can distinguish
    the original conclusion from later evidence.
  </p>

  <h2>Why Commodities</h2>

  <p>
    My interest in markets started with commodities. My mother's side of the
    family comes from a farming background, so weather, harvest conditions and
    commodity prices never felt completely abstract. Learning how futures can
    protect the value of a harvest, manage an airline's fuel exposure or secure
    an industrial input changed how I understood finance.
  </p>

  <p>
    Many of my early reports originated while I served as Commodities Sector
    Head at Queen's Tower Capital, Imperial College London's student-managed
    investment fund. Commodities became an entry point into rates, currencies,
    credit, valuation and broader asset allocation rather than the boundary of
    my research. That wider interest still comes back to the same question: how
    do markets and investing shape exchange, capital allocation and real-world
    outcomes?
  </p>

  <h2>About the Founder</h2>

  <p>
    Sri studies Economics, Finance & Data Science at Imperial College London.
    His interests sit at the intersection of commodity markets, rates,
    currencies, macroeconomics, financial modelling and quantitative analysis.
    SM Research is intended to remain a continuing analytical record rather than
    a substitute for a curriculum vitae.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose my-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
    <Link class="btn-blue" href={withBasePath('/research/')}>
      Read founder research
    </Link>
    <Link class="btn-cyan" href={withBasePath('/contributors/')}>
      Explore contributors
    </Link>
    <Link class="btn-amber" href={withBasePath('/outcomes/')}>
      View decision log
    </Link>
    <Link class="btn-emerald" href={withBasePath('/models/')}>
      Explore models
    </Link>
  </div>

  <h2>Disclosures and Corrections</h2>

  <p>
    All material is provided for educational and informational purposes only and
    does not constitute investment advice. Relevant holdings, incentives and
    conflicts should be disclosed where applicable. Contributor articles carry
    their named author's conclusions. Corrections and dated updates may be
    published when evidence or errors materially change the analysis.
  </p>

  <PageNavigation current="about" />
</div>
